  "response": "## The finding\n\n`Invoice` is declared twice and the two copies disagree on 6 of 11 fields (`crime_00012`, severity 0.96, confidence 0.82):\n\n- `src/api/state.ts:9` — `{ id, totalCents }`\n- `src/billing.ts:19` — `{ id, userId, lines, subtotalCents, discountCents, totalCents, taxCents, currency, createdAt, paymentMethod, metadata }`\n\n`User` is the same shape of problem one size smaller (`crime_00011`): `src/api/state.ts:4` has `{ id, email }`; `src/billing.ts:4` has those plus `plan`, `country`, `createdAt`.\n\nThe load-bearing detail the JSON doesn't say outright: **neither interface is exported.** Both are file-local declarations in files with no import edge between them. That's what `blast_radius: 0` / `blast_radius_direct_importers: 0` actually mean here — not \"nothing depends on this,\" but \"the compiler has no edge on which to compare them.\" Two files each hold a complete-looking, private definition of the same record, and `tsc` is structurally incapable of noticing they differ.\n\n## Which file an agent edits\n\n`src/api/state.ts`, nearly every time. Grep for `interface Invoice` and you get two hits; the agent takes the cheap one:\n\n- 34 lines vs 310, so the whole file fits in one read with room to reason\n- the path says `api/state` — an agent told \"add `status` to the invoice payload\" is already headed there\n- the declaration is 2 fields; it reads as canonical-and-minimal rather than as a stub\n- `billing.ts`'s copy sits at line 19, below a commented-out corpse block (`:33-37`) and above a 204-line god function — expensive to open, expensive to be confident in\n\n## What silently breaks\n\nNothing at compile time, which is the whole problem. The field lands on the API-side type and never reaches `generateInvoice`, the only place that computes `subtotal`, `discountCents`, `taxCents`, and `currency` and assembles the object at `src/billing.ts:168-180`. The API type now advertises a field that no constructed invoice carries.\n\nThe reverse edit is worse. Add the field in `billing.ts` and `src/api/state.ts` stays silently narrower — anything typed against that copy keeps passing `tsc` while missing `userId`, `currency`, and `taxCents` outright. The hole is already open and demonstrable: `src/api/state.ts:24` declares `users: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" }` and type-checks clean, while `src/billing.ts:92` does tenure-discount math on `user.createdAt` — a field the API-side `User` does not know exists. Hand that object to `calculateRefundPreview` and the discount branch reads `undefined`.\n\n## Where it sits, by agent_risk\n\n| Finding | agent_risk |\n|---|---|\n| `contract_drift` — `Invoice` (`crime_00012`) | **0.37** |\n| `contract_drift` — `User` (`crime_00011`) | **0.37** |\n| `large_function` — `generateInvoice`, 204 lines (`crime_00033`) | 0.13 |\n| `large_file` — `src/billing.ts`, 310 lines (`crime_00036`) | 0.07 |\n\nBoth drifts outrank the god function 2.8× and the god file 5.3× on `agent_risk`. Fix them first.\n\nSeverity and line count both order this differently, and both are the wrong instrument. Severity puts `generateInvoice` at 0.90 with 0.95 confidence; line count makes it the headline at 204 lines against the drift's four. But those measure how bad the code is to *hold in your head* — and 204 lines of mess in one contiguous body is a problem an agent can at least *see*, because opening the function shows you all of it. `agent_risk` measures something else: how likely an edit is to be wrong *and* go undetected. Split-brain wins that badly. Each declaration reads as complete and authoritative in isolation, there's no import linking them, and no test, type, or lint rule fires when they diverge further.\n\nTwo caveats worth stating plainly:\n\n- **0.37 is not the top of this report.** Ten findings score higher — `direct_date` in `src/date.ts` at 0.45, `return_shape_roulette` at 0.44, `missing_agent_context` at 0.43. The claim here is ordering against the *structural* findings you asked about, not against the whole scan.\n- **0.37 is probably an undercount.** Its two largest suppressors are `blast_radius: 0` and `churn: 0.05` — and the blast-radius zero is an artifact of the defect itself. Export either interface and have the other reference it, and the metric would rise even as the actual risk fell to zero. Read 0.37 as a floor.\n\nOne structural note on the fixture framing: `src/api/state.ts:1-2` declares itself fixture material not to be imported from real code. In a production repo that header would be grounds to downgrade — here it's the fixture's own scaffolding, and the drift it demonstrates is real.\n\n## Fix\n\nExport the `billing.ts` declarations (the 11-field `Invoice`, the 5-field `User`) as the single source, and have `src/api/state.ts` derive from them rather than restate them — `type Invoice = Pick<BillingInvoice, \"id\" | \"totalCents\">` if the API surface is genuinely meant to be narrower. That converts the next field addition from a silent divergence into a compile error at the projection site, which is the only place it can be correctly decided.",
